---
import BlogPostLayout from "../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro";
import BlogByline from "../../components/BlogByline.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";

// Make this page dynamic via SSR
export const prerender = false;

const publishDate = new Date("July 20 2026");
const readTime = "5 min read";

const isPublished = new Date().getTime() >= publishDate.getTime();

---

<BlogPostLayout
  title="What Military Precision Actually Means in Your House"
  schemaTitle="What 'Military Precision' Actually Means in Your House"
  description={isPublished ? "Every veteran-owned cleaning company says 'military precision.' Here's what it actually means: a written 44-item checklist, the same order every visit, and photos of the finished work." : "Coming Soon: What 'Military Precision' Actually Means in Your House"}
  noindex={!isPublished}
  publishDate={publishDate}
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="pt-24 pb-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-900 via-gray-800 to-gray-900">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="text-center">
        <span class="inline-block px-4 py-2 bg-[#FFA985]/20 text-[#FFA985] rounded-full text-sm font-medium mb-6">
          Brand Story/Trust
        </span>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-bold text-white mb-6 leading-tight">
          What "Military Precision" Actually Means in Your House
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          {isPublished ? "It's not a slogan. It's a checklist." : "Coming July 20 2026."}
        </p>
        <div class="mb-8"><BlogByline variant="dark" date={publishDate} readTime={readTime} /></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Main Content Toggle based on Published Date -->
  {isPublished ? (
    <article class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <h2>Every veteran-owned cleaning company says "military precision." Here's what it actually means.</h2>
<p><strong>At The Valley Clean Team, it means one specific thing: a written 44-item checklist, run in the same order on every single visit. Todd, our founder, is an Air Force veteran, and the one habit the Air Force drills into everyone is this — you don't trust memory. You trust the checklist.</strong></p>

<h3>Where the checklist habit comes from</h3>
<p>In 1935, the most experienced test pilots in the Army Air Corps crashed a brand-new B-17 on takeoff because one control lock was still engaged. The airplane wasn't too hard to fly. It was too much to remember. The fix wasn't more training or better pilots — it was a piece of paper: the pre-flight checklist. Aviation has run on them ever since.</p>
<p>The lesson isn't that pilots are careless. It's that everyone forgets things, especially when the work is routine. A cleaner on their fourth house of the day is in exactly that spot. That's why we don't rely on anyone's memory — including our own.</p>
<p>(Pilots don't skip the checklist on a sunny day. Neither do we.)</p>

<h3>What that looks like in your house</h3>
<p>Every regular visit runs the same written 44-item checklist. Deep cleans run 57 items. The order matters, too: we work top to bottom, because dust falls — there's no point mopping a floor before the ceiling fan gets dusted. Blinds before sills, counters before floors, dry work before wet work.</p>
<p>The result is that the clean doesn't depend on who's on the crew that day or what they happen to notice. The checklist notices for them. That's the whole trick — and it's why the house looks the same after visit twenty as it did after visit one.</p>

<h3>The other half: owning the result</h3>
<p>The second habit that followed Todd out of the service is simpler: say what you'll do, do it, and own what happened. In practice that means we show up when we said we would, and if we missed something, you tell us within 24 hours and we'll get a re-clean on the schedule right away, at no charge. You shouldn't have to chase your cleaning company for an answer. We think that's weird.</p>

<h3>The honest math</h3>
<p>Regular cleaning is from $200. Your square footage and the type of cleaning set the price — not an hourly rate, not a guess. The number we quote is the number we bill, and it moves in exactly two situations, both yours: you add something to the job, or the home details we quoted from need correcting. Either way, you'll hear from us before anything changes.</p>

<h3>Want it handled?</h3>
<p><strong>Call (256) 826-1100, tell us your square footage and the type of cleaning, and you'll have your exact price before you hang up.</strong> Background-checked cleaners, $2 million in liability insurance, and a written checklist on every visit — that's what "military precision" means around here.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  ) : (
    <article class="py-16 bg-white border-t border-gray-100 shadow-inner">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-center">
          <Icon name="clock" class="w-16 h-16 text-[#FFA985] mx-auto mb-6" />
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-4 mt-0">Article Coming Soon!</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">This article is locked and scheduled to automatically publish on <br/><strong class="text-gray-800 text-xl">July 20 2026</strong>.<br/>Check back then!</p>
          <div class="mt-12">
            <a href="/blog" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 text-lg font-semibold text-white bg-[#FFA985] rounded-full hover:bg-[#ff9a70] transition shadow-md no-underline">
              Back to Blog
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  )}
</BlogPostLayout>
